v8 ADDITIONS - two x86 vector-insertion inefficiencies fixed
=====================================================================

(1) gcc/expmed.cc - store_bit_field_1: use vec_set even when the stored
    field's mode differs from the vector's element mode.

    __m256i and __m512i are vectors of *long long*, so the ubiquitous
    intrinsic idiom

        ((int *) &v)[i] = x;

    presents an SImode field in a V4DI/V8DI vector.  store_bit_field_1
    only tried vec_set when fieldmode == innermode, so this fell all the
    way through to "spill the whole vector to an aligned stack slot,
    poke the memory, load it back".  The new code retypes OP0 with
    related_vector_mode () to the vector mode whose element *is*
    fieldmode (V4DI -> V8SI) and uses that vec_set.  The subreg is a
    free reinterpretation of a same-size register, and because SUB is a
    subreg of OP0 the vec_set writes directly into OP0 - no copy back
    (an earlier version emitted one and miscompiled; caught by the
    runtime test below).

    Measured, -O2 -march=x86-64-v4, instructions per function:

        idiom                       before   after
        ((int*)&m256i)[0]   = 0        11       3
        ((int*)&m256i)[1]   = 0         9       3
        ((int*)&m256i)[4]   = 0        11       4
        ((int*)&m256i)[7]   = 0         9       4
        ((int*)&m256i)[2]   = x        13       3
        ((short*)&m256i)[5] = 0        11       4
        ((char*)&m256i)[9]  = 0        10       4
        ((int*)&m256d)[3]   = 0         9       3
        ((int*)&m512i)[6]   = 0        11       5

    i.e. 55-77% fewer instructions, and the stack frame disappears
    entirely (no push %rbp / andq $-32,%rsp / vmovdqa spill+reload).

(2) gcc/config/i386/i386-expand.cc - ix86_expand_vector_set: zero a
    64-bit lane of V2DI/V2DF with insertps.

    A 64-bit lane covers exactly two 32-bit lanes, so zeroing it is
    expressible on the V4SI view: element ELT covers dword lanes 2*ELT
    and 2*ELT+1, giving a keep-mask of (3 << 2*ELT) ^ 15, which is 12
    for ELT==0 and 3 for ELT==1 - both inside the 1..14 range that
    sse4_1_insertps_<mode>_zero requires.  This removes the
    xorl %eax,%eax + vpinsrq pair:

        v2di v[0] = 0   :  3 insns -> 2  (vinsertps $3)
        v2df v[0] = 0.0 :  3 insns -> 2  (vinsertps $3)

    V8HI/V16QI are deliberately NOT handled: a 16- or 8-bit lane is only
    part of a 32-bit lane, so insertps cannot express it and there is no
    cheaper sequence than the existing vpinsrw/vpinsrb.

    Also hardened: the avx512 blendm path in ix86_expand_vector_set now
    force_regs an immediate VAL before building the VEC_DUPLICATE, and
    the V_256_512 vec_set expander accepts reg_or_0_operand to match the
    V_128 one.

VALIDATION
    A full cc1 was configured and built from this tree (make all-gcc,
    RC=0) and used for everything below.

    Runtime correctness, comparing against a memcpy-built reference for
    every lane of __m128i/__m256i/__m512i/__m256d written through int*,
    short*, char* and long long* pointers:

        -O0, -O1, -O2 -march=raptorlake, -O3 -march=raptorlake,
        -O2 -march=x86-64-v4, -Os, -O3 -mavx2,
        -msse4.1 / -mavx / -mavx2 / -mavx512f+bw+vl
        => 46-55 checks each, 0 failures

    The unpatched r581 compiler was run as a control on the same test
    and also passes, so the test is not vacuous.  (An earlier revision
    of the test used a *variable* lane index; GCC deletes such a
    type-punned store as UB, which made it pass spuriously.  The final
    test uses constant indices only.)

    Randomised torture: 20000 iterations x 2 vectors of interleaved
    punned writes (int/short/char/long long at assorted constant lanes)
    compared byte-exactly against memcpy references - 40000 checks, 0
    failures at -O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os and with x86-64-v4.

    No ICEs compiling gcc/tree.cc and gcc/fold-const.cc with the patched
    compiler at -O2 -march=raptorlake.

    New regression tests:
        gcc.target/i386/ms178-vecset-retype-1.c
        gcc.target/i386/ms178-vecset-zero-2di-1.c
    Both were checked to satisfy their dg-final assertions with the
    patched compiler (0 stack-frame insns; 0 pinsrq; 2 insertps).

