v9 ADDITIONS - loop vectorizer cost-model and dependence-analysis audit
=====================================================================

Nine defects, all in the same class: 32-bit / signed-unsigned arithmetic
on quantities that are neither bounded to 32 bits nor unsigned-safe.

--- A. gcc/tree-vect-loop.cc, vect_estimate_min_profitable_iters ----

vec_inside_cost, vec_prologue_cost and vec_epilogue_cost are unsigned
accumulations of per-statement costs.  Each statement cost is
  builtin_vectorization_cost * count
and, for statements in an inner loop, is further multiplied by
LOOP_VINFO_INNER_LOOP_COST_FACTOR, i.e. --param=vect-inner-loop-cost-factor,
whose documented range is 1..10000.  Nothing saturates.  The function then
mixed those unsigned values into int expressions:

  1. vec_outside_cost = (int)(vec_prologue_cost + vec_epilogue_cost);
     Truncation; can make the outside cost appear negative.
     -> compute in unsigned HOST_WIDE_INT and saturate to INT_MAX.

  2. int saving_per_viter = scalar_single_iter_cost * assumed_vf
                            - vec_inside_cost;
     "int - unsigned" is evaluated as *unsigned*, so a large negative
     saving becomes a large positive one and the "saving_per_viter <= 0"
     bail-out is skipped - the loop is then costed as profitable when it
     is not.
     -> compute in signed HOST_WIDE_INT.

  3. min_profitable_iters = (voc - soc) * vf
                            - vec_inside_cost * peel_iters_prologue
                            - vec_inside_cost * peel_iters_epilogue;
     Same unsigned contamination, plus the products themselves overflow
     int (peel counts go up to the vectorization factor).  The "<= 0"
     clamp is skipped and the garbage value is then divided.
     -> signed HOST_WIDE_INT throughout, saturate on the way back to int.
     Note the original already casts to (int) two lines further down,
     which shows the mixing was known to be delicate there but was missed
     on the assignment itself.

  4. min_profitable_estimate: identical shape, and it *recomputed* the
     divisor as "(scalar_single_iter_cost * assumed_vf) - vec_inside_cost"
     in unsigned arithmetic instead of reusing saving_per_viter, which the
     bail-out above has already proven positive.
     -> reuse saving_per_viter, evaluate in 64-bit, clamp at 0 and INT_MAX.

  5./6. both "threshold = vec_inside_cost * min_vec_niters + ..." sites:
     the product overflows int for large bodies.
     -> 64-bit with saturation.

  7./8. both "min_vec_niters = outside_overhead / saving_per_viter + 1"
     sites: saving_per_viter is now 64-bit; keep the quotient 64-bit and
     saturate.

  9. The dump printed vec_inside_cost / vec_prologue_cost /
     vec_epilogue_cost - all unsigned - with %d.  A format mismatch that
     also misreports any cost above INT_MAX as a negative number.
     -> %u.

--- B. gcc/tree-vect-data-refs.cc, dependence distances ------------

  10./11. "int dist = dist_v[loop_depth];" at two sites.  dist_v elements
     are lambda_int, which is HOST_WIDE_INT.  Truncating to int can turn a
     true distance of 2^32 into 0, and 0 is interpreted as "same location";
     it can also turn 0x100000004 into 4 and needlessly clamp max_vf.
     -> keep the full HOST_WIDE_INT width (and print with
        HOST_WIDE_INT_PRINT_DEC).

  12. "unsigned int abs_dist = abs (dist);"  abs (INT_MIN) is undefined
     behaviour, and on a HOST_WIDE_INT distance plain abs() also truncates.
     If the UB is folded to INT_MIN the unsigned result is 2^31, which
     compares >= *max_vf and makes the dependence be *ignored*.
     -> absu_hwi (), which is defined for the most negative value and
        returns unsigned HOST_WIDE_INT.

  13. STMT_VINFO_MIN_NEG_DIST is an unsigned int; assigning a
     HOST_WIDE_INT distance to it truncates, which would under-estimate
     the dependence.
     -> saturate at UINT_MAX.

WHY THIS SHAPE OF FIX
    The costs are genuinely unbounded, so the choice is between bounding
    them at the source (invasive: every target's add_stmt_cost hook would
    have to saturate) or making the consumer overflow-proof.  Doing the
    arithmetic in HOST_WIDE_INT and saturating only where the value must
    re-enter an int interface is the smaller, local change, keeps every
    in-range result bit-identical, and matches how the surrounding code
    already handles niters (poly_uint64 / HOST_WIDE_INT).

VALIDATION
    * cc1 rebuilt from this tree: RC=0, and the two touched files compile
      with no new warnings (notably no -Wsign-compare from the widening).

    * Differential model check: the exact arithmetic of
      vect_estimate_min_profitable_iters, before and after, compared
      against an __int128 reference over a 26,880-point sweep of
      (vec_outside_cost, scalar_outside_cost, assumed_vf, vec_inside_cost,
      peel counts, scalar_single_iter_cost):
          patched disagrees with exact : 0
          patched bail-out mismatches  : 0
          original disagrees with exact: 3920
      i.e. the fix is exact everywhere the original was right, and fixes
      3920 cases where it was wrong.

    * Whole gcc.dg/vect testsuite compiled with stock r581 and with the
      patched compiler at -O3 -march=raptorlake, comparing the number of
      "loop vectorized" messages per file:
          2001 files, 0 differing, 0 ICEs
      So no vectorization decision changes in the normal range - the fix
      is behaviour-preserving where the original did not overflow.

    * Cost-model dumps compared line by line on a mixed float/int/double
      kernel: identical ("Vector inside of loop cost", "Calculated minimum
      iters for profitability").

    * Runtime: 345 executable gcc.dg/vect tests built and run, 0 failures.
      (One further file, pr101145.c, fails to build in this harness for
      both compilers once the include path is equalised - a missing
      linux/limits.h in the sandbox, not a code difference.)

    * A mixed kernel (float add, int mul, double FMA, float dot-product
      reduction, int reduction, min/max) produces bit-identical results
      to stock at -O2/-O3/-march=x86-64-v4, and at -Ofast differs only in
      the FP-reassociated dot product, as expected.

    * New regression test:
          gcc.dg/vect/ms178-cost-overflow-1.c
      compiles without ICE at --param=vect-inner-loop-cost-factor=
      1 / 2 / 5000 / 10000 and still reports the expected vectorization.

=====================================================================
v10 ADDITIONS - peeling-for-alignment audit
=====================================================================

Two further defects in gcc/tree-vect-data-refs.cc, both of the same
family as the v9 cost-model findings: a value of one type stored or
compared as another.

--- 14. vect_peeling_hash_choose_best_peeling: wrong sentinel --------

    res.inside_cost  = INT_MAX;
    res.outside_cost = INT_MAX;

_vect_peel_extended_info::inside_cost and ::outside_cost are
"unsigned int".  Seeding the running minimum with INT_MAX (2^31-1)
rather than UINT_MAX means any peeling candidate whose cost lands in
(INT_MAX, UINT_MAX] loses the

    if (inside_cost < min->inside_cost || ...)

comparison unconditionally - even when it is the only candidate.  The
traversal then leaves res.peel_info.dr_info == NULL and the caller
concludes that no peeling option exists, silently giving up on aligning
the accesses.  Costs are unbounded accumulations (see the v9 notes on
--param=vect-inner-loop-cost-factor, range 1..10000), so this is the
same reachability argument as before.

    -> seed with UINT_MAX, which is the maximum of the field's actual
       type.  Note the fix cannot be "make the fields int": they are
       compared against, and assigned from, unsigned cost accumulators.

--- 15. vect_peeling_supportable: DR_STEP kept in an unsigned int ----

    unsigned int dr0_step = tree_to_shwi (DR_STEP (dr0_info->dr));
    ...
    if (tree_to_shwi (DR_STEP (dr)) != dr0_step)
      return peeling_unsupported;

DR_STEP is signed and is not bounded to 32 bits.  Storing it in an
unsigned int does two wrong things at once:

  * a negative step becomes a huge positive number, so the comparison
    against the signed tree_to_shwi () value on the other side is false
    even when both references have exactly the same step.  Descending
    loops therefore reported "different alignment steps" and returned
    PEELING_UNSUPPORTED - a missed optimisation, not a miscompile.
    Demonstrated: step -8 stored as 4294967288, compared against -8.

  * a step wider than 32 bits truncates (0x100000008 -> 8).

    -> keep it as HOST_WIDE_INT, matching what tree_to_shwi () returns
       and what DR_STEP actually is.

REACHABILITY - stated honestly
    Both sites sit behind "supportable_dr_alignment ==
    dr_unaligned_unsupported".  x86 reports unaligned accesses as
    supported almost everywhere, so I could not construct an x86 test
    that reaches the divergent comparison: instrumenting the compiler
    and running the whole gcc.dg/vect suite hit the comparison 40 times,
    all with small positive steps, and 0 of those diverged between the
    old and new expressions.  These are therefore latent defects - they
    matter for strict-alignment targets and for any future x86 path that
    returns dr_unaligned_unsupported - and the fixes are provably
    behaviour-neutral for the cases that do occur today.  I am not
    claiming a user-visible x86 speedup for them.

    I also audited and explicitly did NOT change:
      * epilog->nb_iterations_upper_bound -= lowest_vf
        (tree-vect-loop-manip.cc).  Potential unsigned underflow, but
        instrumenting the subtraction and scanning all 2002 gcc.dg/vect
        files produced 0 underflow events, so the documented invariant
        ("the vector loop covered at least VF scalar iterations") holds.
        No change made.
      * misal += npeel * TREE_INT_CST_LOW (DR_STEP (...)) at three sites.
        The following "&= alignment - 1" makes the result correct
        regardless of any wrapping.  No change made.
      * int inside_cost/prologue_cost in vect_model_simple_cost and
        vect_model_promotion_demotion_cost.  These feed only dump output,
        not a decision.  No change made.
      * (group_size * cvf - gap) % cnunits with ceil_log2 (0): ceil_log2
        is defined to return 0 for 0, so cpart_size becomes 1.  Handled.
        No change made.

VALIDATION
    * cc1 rebuilt: RC=0, no new warnings from the touched file.
    * Whole gcc.dg/vect suite compiled stock vs patched at
      -O3 -march=native, comparing "loop vectorized" counts per file:
          2002 files, 0 differing, 0 ICEs.
    * Runtime: 855 executable gcc.dg/vect tests built and run, 0 failures.
      (An earlier sweep at -march=raptorlake showed 6 "Illegal
      instruction" failures; the stock r581 compiler fails those 6
      identically, and all 6 pass at -march=native - the sandbox host
      lacks AVX-VNNI.  Not a code difference.)
    * Sentinel and step-comparison mechanics each demonstrated in a
      standalone model: candidate cost 3000000000 is rejected with the
      INT_MAX sentinel and accepted with UINT_MAX; step -8 compares
      unequal to itself through an unsigned int and equal through
      HOST_WIDE_INT.
    * New regression test gcc.dg/vect/ms178-peel-sentinel-1.c: no ICE at
      --param=vect-inner-loop-cost-factor=1 and 10000, still vectorizes.

=====================================================================
v11 ADDITIONS - SSE / AVX / AVX2 / BMI2 / scalar codegen audit
=====================================================================

Two defects, one of them a dead pattern shipped by v10 itself.  Both
were found empirically (compile a corpus of idioms, read the asm, then
instrument the compiler to prove reachability) rather than by reading
code, and both are validated by differential sweeps whose *control is
the v10 compiler*, so every number below is attributable to v11 alone.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
16. gcc/expmed.cc, store_bit_field_1: insert a whole SUB-VECTOR with
    vec_init instead of spilling the vector to the stack.
---------------------------------------------------------------------

SYMPTOM

    Writing a 128-bit value into one half of a 256-bit vector - the
    single most common shape in hand-written SIMD code, and the shape
    the vectorizer itself emits whenever it builds a wide vector out of
    two half-width results - generated a full stack round-trip:

        __m256i f (__m256i r, __m128i hi)
        { memcpy ((char *) &r + 16, &hi, 16); return r; }

          push  %rbp                 <- frame, only to get 32-byte
          mov   %rsp,%rbp               alignment for the spill slot
          and   $-32,%rsp
          vmovdqa %ymm0,-32(%rsp)
          vmovdqa %xmm1,-16(%rsp)
          vmovdqa -32(%rsp),%ymm0
          leave
          ret

    where the machine has exactly one instruction for this:

          vinserti128 $0x1,%xmm1,%ymm0,%ymm0

ROOT CAUSE

    store_bit_field_1 has a vec_set path, but vec_set only takes a
    *scalar* field, so a vector-valued field skips it and falls through
    to the generic "store a full-size field with a SUBREG" path.  That
    path emits

        (set (subreg:V4SI (reg:V8SI R) 16) X)

    i.e. a partial write to a multi-lane vector register.  There is no
    mov alternative that can satisfy such an operand, so LRA resolves it
    the only way it can - it spills the whole vector, stores the lane to
    memory and reloads.  Confirmed in the LRA dump:

        Considering alt=3 of insn 8: (0) v (1) vm
           Cycle danger: overall += LRA_MAX_REJECT
        Choosing alt 4 in insn 8: (0) m (1) v {movv4si_internal}

    The "v"->"v" register alternative is rejected outright and the
    memory alternative wins.  The frame setup is pure collateral: the
    spill slot needs 32-byte alignment, which forces and $-32,%rsp and
    therefore a frame pointer, in a function that otherwise needs no
    stack at all.

FIX

    Recognise the case before the subreg path and rebuild the value with
    vec_init from its two halves: extract the half that is being kept
    with vec_extract, then vec_init the kept half and the new half into
    a fresh register.  On x86 the extraction of the low half is free (a
    subreg) and of the high half is one vextracti128, and the vec_init
    is the single vinsert the machine wanted all along.

    Deliberately restricted to the exactly-two-pieces case
    (GET_MODE_SIZE (outer) == 2 * GET_MODE_SIZE (field)).  With three or
    more pieces the extractions could cost more than the spill they
    replace, so that case is left to the existing code.  Also gated on
    !reverse (storage-order flips must not silently go through a lane
    shuffle) and on GET_MODE_INNER (field) == GET_MODE_INNER (outer), so
    the two modes are genuinely the same vector cut in half.  If the
    vec_init fails to expand, delete_insns_since () unwinds cleanly and
    the old path still runs.

MEASURED (-O2 -march=native, instructions per function)

        idiom                                    v10   v11
        memcpy into upper half of __m256i         8     1
        memcpy into lower half of __m256i         8     1
        memcpy into upper half of __m256d         8     1
        (__m128 *) &v256)[1] = x                  8     1
        memcpy into upper half of __m512i         8     1
        two vpmovsxwd halves -> __m256i          11     4

    In every case the stack frame disappears entirely.  Note the last
    row: that is the shape the vectorizer produces for a plain widening
    loop body, so this is not only an intrinsics fix.

CORRECTNESS

    gcc.target/i386/ms178-subvec-insert-1.c is included, and a runtime
    harness checks 21 distinct insertion shapes - {256,512}-bit outer
    with {128,256}-bit fields, plus 128-bit outer with 64-bit fields,
    for int / float / double / long long / short / char element types,
    both halves - against a byte-exact memcpy reference, 200 random
    vectors each.  All pass at -O0, -O1, -O2, -O3, -Os, and at
    -march=native, x86-64-v3, x86-64-v2 and -mprefer-vector-width=512.

    An earlier revision of this hunk emitted a write-back move after the
    insert; it was caught by the same harness and removed.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
17. gcc/config/i386/i386.md: the v10 MULX highpart pattern was DEAD
    CODE.  Reach it from the optab expander.
---------------------------------------------------------------------

v10 added

    (define_insn "*bmi2_umul<mode>3_highpart" ...)

intending to get MULX for an unsigned highpart-only multiply.  It never
matched even once.  A "*"-prefixed define_insn has no generator function
and is only ever found by recog () on RTL that some other pass already
built - and nothing in the compiler ever builds a bare umul_highpart
whose operand 1 is already sitting in RDX.  The optab-named pattern
<s>mul<mode>3_highpart is what expand actually calls, and it hardcodes
the RAX/RDX MUL form, so that is what was always emitted:

        u64 hi (u64 a, u64 b) { return (unsigned __int128) a * b >> 64; }
          mov %rdi,%rax ; mul %rsi ; mov %rdx,%rax      <- v10, 3 insns

Verified by grepping the generated insn-recog.cc / insn-output.cc of a
v10 build and by compiling the idiom at -march=raptorlake, x86-64-v3
and x86-64-v2: no MULX at any of them.

FIX

    Turn <s>mul<mode>3_highpart into a define_expand that, for the
    unsigned case with TARGET_BMI2, emits a *non*-starred
    bmi2_umul<mode>3_highpart; the old MUL insn is renamed
    *<s>mul<mode>3_highpart_1 and keeps serving everything else.  Now
    register allocation is responsible for getting operand 1 into RDX,
    which it does with exactly the one move that MUL's "%a" constraint
    would have required anyway - so the eliminated "mov %rdx,%dst" is
    pure profit.  The u_bool code attribute is extended to the
    {s,u}mul_highpart codes so the expander can distinguish them; the
    signed case has no MULX equivalent and continues to use IMUL.

    MULX also writes no flags, so the result is one fewer EFLAGS
    clobber in the middle of a dependence chain.

MEASURED

        (unsigned __int128) a * b >> 64        3 insns -> 2
        a * *p >> 64            (memory rhs)   3 insns -> 2
        (__int128) a * b >> 64  (signed)       unchanged, still IMUL

    In the testsuite, gcc.target/i386/mult-highpart.c drops from 160 to
    153 instructions, and gcc.dg/vect/vect-div-bitmask-3.c loses one
    instruction from its inner loop (3 -> 1 there) while the cold
    epilogue grows by the same amount, netting zero on static count but
    winning where it is executed.

CORRECTNESS

    gcc.target/i386/ms178-mulx-highpart-1.c is included.  A runtime
    harness cross-checks hi64, hi32, signed hi64, a memory-operand form,
    a both-halves form, a highpart feeding a flags-consuming compare and
    a highpart feeding an add-with-overflow chain against __int128
    references: all 121 pairs of interesting edge values (0, 1, 2, ~0,
    2^63, 2^32, ...) plus 3,000,000 random pairs per function - about 18
    million comparisons - with 0 mismatches, at -O0, -O1, -O2, -O3, -Os,
    at -march=raptorlake / native / x86-64, and with -mno-bmi2.

    MULX's "both destinations equal yields the high half" behaviour was
    not taken on trust; it was verified directly on the host with inline
    asm over the same edge cases and 2,000,000 random pairs.

