=====================================================================
v11 ADDITION #18 - scalar FP clamp: phiopt rejected the UNORDERED
compare codes, so Mesa's CLAMP()/SATURATE() kept a branch
=====================================================================

FOUND BY

Reading the user's own mesa-git patch first.  It replaces the plain-C
float_to_ubyte()/float_to_ushort() in src/util/u_math.h with hand-written
_mm_max_ss/_mm_min_ss intrinsics.  Someone only does that when the
compiler is getting the C version wrong, so that was the thread to pull.

SYMPTOM

    /* src/util/macros.h:375, verbatim */
    #define CLAMP(X, MIN, MAX) ((X)>(MIN) ? ((X)>(MAX) ? (MAX) : (X)) : (MIN))
    #define SATURATE(X) CLAMP(X, 0.0f, 1.0f)

    float hi (float x) { return x > 1.0f ? 1.0f : x; }

  compiled with the user's Mesa flags (-O3 -march=native ...
  -fno-math-errno -fno-trapping-math) gave

        vucomiss .LC0(%rip), %xmm0
        vmovd    %xmm0, %eax          <- float leaves the vector unit
        cmova    .LC0(%rip), %eax     <- ... to use an integer cmov ...
        vmovd    %eax, %xmm0          <- ... and comes back

  instead of the two instructions the machine actually has:

        vmovss   .LC0(%rip), %xmm1
        vminss   %xmm0, %xmm1, %xmm0

  Two extra cross-domain moves (each ~1-3 cycles of bypass delay on
  Raptor Lake) on an idiom Mesa uses at 572 call sites in 150 files.

ROOT CAUSE

  tree-ssa-phiopt.cc, match_simplify_replacement, the "Non-IEEE FP
  MIN/MAX PHI replacement" fallback.  When match.pd cannot produce a
  MIN_EXPR/MAX_EXPR (it cannot here, because with NaNs honoured
  `x > 1.0f ? 1.0f : x` is NOT IEEE fmin), this fallback still rebuilds
  the PHI as a COND_EXPR so the backend can pick minss/maxss - which
  implement exactly the non-IEEE "a < b ? b : a" semantics.

  But it gated on

      if (cmp != LT_EXPR && cmp != LE_EXPR
          && cmp != GT_EXPR && cmp != GE_EXPR)
        return false;

  and a clamp written the other way round never has one of those codes.
  Inverting an ordered FP compare while HONOR_NANS is true must yield
  the *unordered* complement, so by the time phiopt sees it the IL says

      if (x_2(D) u>= 0.0)  goto bb3; else goto bb4;
      # _1 = PHI <0.0(3), x_2(D)(2)>

  i.e. UNGE, which the gate rejects.  The branch therefore survived into
  RTL, where if-conversion had no FP conditional-move to use and fell
  back to bouncing the value through a GPR.  Confirmed from the
  -fdump-tree-phiopt1-all dump:

      phiopt match-simplify trying:
          x_2(D) u>= 0.0 ? x_2(D) : 0.0     <- no match.pd rule
      (and then the fallback declines because the code is UNGE)

FIX

  Accept the four unordered codes and normalise each to its ordered
  complement with the two arms swapped:

      UNGE -> LT (swap)    UNGT -> LE (swap)
      UNLE -> GT (swap)    UNLT -> GE (swap)

  That is an identity, not an approximation: the unordered predicates
  are *defined* as the negations of the opposite ordered ones, for every
  input including NaN.  After normalisation the expression is in exactly
  the form the existing code already emits, so nothing downstream -
  neither the min/max recognisers nor ix86_expand_sse_fp_minmax - has to
  learn about the unordered spellings.

  Note this only changes which *spelling* reaches expand.  It does not
  relax any NaN or signed-zero rule: the surrounding
  `if (!HONOR_NANS (type) && !HONOR_SIGNED_ZEROS (type)) return false;`
  guard, and the requirement that the PHI arms be exactly the two
  compare operands, are both untouched.

WHY -fno-trapping-math MATTERS

  The bug is only observable with -fno-trapping-math (which the user
  has).  With trapping math the comparison cannot be speculated and a
  branch survives for an unrelated reason, so the missing minss is
  invisible.  The included regression test therefore passes
  -fno-trapping-math explicitly; without it the test would pass
  vacuously on the unfixed compiler.

CORRECTNESS

  * The four identities were model-checked exhaustively over a pool of
    22 "interesting" floats (both zeros, both min subnormals, both min
    normals, +-1, +-2, +-0.5, +-max, +-inf, two qNaNs with different
    payloads, an sNaN): 1600 pair-wise comparisons of raw bit patterns,
    0 mismatches.  Bit patterns rather than == so that -0.0 vs +0.0 and
    NaN payloads are actually distinguished.
  * A runtime harness checks 10 clamp/min/max spellings plus Mesa's real
    CLAMP/SATURATE macros against volatile references over the same
    pool, including all 22^3 = 10648 triples for the 3-argument clamp:
    12738 checks, 0 mismatches, at -O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os and at
    -march=native and -march=x86-64.
    (Under -ffast-math the harness reports mismatches, but the STOCK
    r581 compiler reports the identical count on the identical harness -
    that is -ffast-math legitimately assuming no NaNs, not a regression.
    The user does not build Mesa with -ffast-math.)

MEASURED - control is a v11 compiler with ONLY this hunk reverted

  A second complete cc1 was built from the same tree with just this
  change backed out, so every number below is attributable to it alone.

  Mesa shapes, user's flags (-O3 -march=native -mtune=native
  -fno-math-errno -fno-trapping-math -mprefer-vector-width=256
  -funroll-loops -falign-functions=32):

        file            insns          per-function highlights
        mesareal.c    765 -> 702       clamp_color_range  56 -> 34  (-39%)
                                       saturate_buf      199 -> 162  (-19%)
                                       clamp_color        50 -> 48
                                       vp_clamp            9 ->  7
        mesaclamp.c   239 -> 199
        mm.c           67 ->  60
        codes.c        38 ->  30

  In the vectorized clamp loop the improvement is structural, not just a
  smaller count - a compare plus two masked blends collapses into one
  masked min:

        base:  vcmpps $10,...,%k2 ; vcmpps $14,...,%k1
               vmovaps %xmm2,%xmm1{%k2} ; vmovaps %xmm1,%xmm0{%k1}
        fix:   vcmpps $14,...,%k1 ; vminps %xmm2,%xmm1,%xmm0{%k1}

  Wall clock, 4096-element saturate loop, 30000 iterations, data
  deliberately straddling both clamp bounds so the baseline's branch is
  unpredictable (best of 5, three independent runs):

        baseline  0.076 ns/element
        fixed     0.039 ns/element      1.95x faster, reproducible

  Whole-Mesa scan (all files that build standalone in the sandbox, 176
  of them, compared against the same control): 3 files change, all
  improvements, net -115 instructions and -31 branches, 0 regressions.
  The reach is understated by the sandbox - most of the 572
  CLAMP/SATURATE call sites live in files that need a configured Mesa
  build tree to compile.

REGRESSION VALIDATION - same control

