=====================================================================
v11 ADDITION #19 - bit-field arithmetic blocked loop vectorization,
which is exactly how ACO's and NIR's packed IR nodes are written
=====================================================================

FOUND BY

Extracting ACO's real data structures and hot loops from
src/amd/compiler/ and compiling them standalone with the user's Mesa
flags, then diffing the vector-instruction count against the same loops
written on a plain uint32_t array with explicit shifts and masks.  The
"abstraction-free" control vectorized; the real ACO code did not.

SYMPTOM

  ACO packs its central IR value type into one word:

      struct Temp {                        /* aco_ir.h */
        uint32_t id_       : 24;
        uint32_t reg_class : 8;
      };

  and RegClass::bytes()/size()/type()/is_linear() all do arithmetic on
  the 8-bit field.  Loops over arrays of Temp - which is most of the
  register allocator, the validator and the schedulers - came out fully
  scalar:

      unsigned total_size (const Temp *t, unsigned n)
      { unsigned s = 0; for (...) s += t[i].size (); return s; }

        v11 baseline :  90 insns,   0 vector instructions
        with this fix:  99 insns,  99 vector instructions

  The identical computation written as (t[i] >> 24) on a uint32_t array
  vectorized perfectly on both compilers, which is what pointed at the
  bit-field rather than at the arithmetic.

ROOT CAUSE

  if-conversion lowers a bit-field load into a load of the containing
  word plus BIT_FIELD_REF <word, width, pos> (tree-if-conv.cc,
  lower_bitfield).  The vectorizer has a pattern recogniser that turns
  that BIT_FIELD_REF back into a shift-and-mask -
  vect_recog_bitfield_ref_pattern - but it only triggers for two
  consumers:

      * a CONVERT of the BIT_FIELD_REF result, or
      * a gcond comparing it against an INTEGER_CST.

  Any other consumer leaves the BIT_FIELD_REF in the IL, and SLP build
  then rejects it outright:

      missed: Build SLP failed: BIT_FIELD_REF not supported
      missed: step unknown.

  (tree-vect-slp.cc:~1332 - that path only accepts a BIT_FIELD_REF whose
  base is an SSA vector, not a scalar loaded from memory.)  The whole
  loop then stays scalar.

  The two consumers that matter in practice are exactly the ones that
  were missing: BIT_AND_EXPR (every flag test: rc & 32) and a comparison
  against a non-constant.  Minimal reproducer in plain C, no C++:

      struct A { uint32_t x : 8; uint32_t y : 24; };
      s += a[i].x;            /* CONVERT   -> vectorized  */
      s += a[i].x + 1u;       /* PLUS      -> vectorized  */
      s += a[i].x & 32;       /* BIT_AND   -> SCALAR      */
      s += a[i].x > 3;        /* compare   -> SCALAR      */

FIX

  Let the recogniser match the BIT_FIELD_REF assignment on its own, not
  only a CONVERT or gcond wrapping it.  Everything else in the function
  is reused unchanged: it already knows how to emit the shift, the mask,
  the sign-extension variant for signed fields, and how to pick
  mask-then-shift versus shift-then-mask for the widening cases.  A
  BIT_FIELD_REF of an integral container *is* a shift and a mask, so
  lowering it unconditionally costs nothing when the loop does not
  vectorize and unblocks it when it does.

  Three lines of matching, no new code paths.

MEASURED - control is a v11 compiler with ONLY this hunk reverted

  A second complete cc1/cc1plus was built from the same tree with the
  change backed out, so every number is attributable to it alone.

  Real ACO, compiled with the user's Mesa flags (-O3 -march=native
  -mtune=native -fno-semantic-interposition -falign-functions=32
  -fno-math-errno -fno-trapping-math -funroll-loops
  -mprefer-vector-width=256 -fivopts -fomit-frame-pointer
  -malign-data=cacheline -fno-plt -fvisibility=hidden), against the real
  generated aco_opcodes.h/aco_builder.h:

      file                              insns          vector insns
      aco_register_allocation.cpp   56748 / 56538      1428 / 1326
      aco_validate.cpp              17947 / 17618       546 /  491
      aco_lower_branches.cpp         9722 /  9432       784 /  652
      aco_scheduler_ilp.cpp          5133 /  5127        64 /   64

  aco_register_allocation.cpp gains one newly vectorized loop at
  line 2199 - the `for (Definition& def : instr->definitions)
  ... def.getTemp().regClass().is_linear()` scan, which runs for every
  instruction the allocator touches.

  Wall clock on the extracted ACO loops (2048 Temps, 200000 iterations,
  best of 5, two independent runs, both agreeing to <1%):

      loop            baseline      fixed        speedup
      size()          0.689 ns/e    0.180 ns/e    3.8x
      type()          0.310 ns/e    0.155 ns/e    2.0x
      is_linear()     0.317 ns/e    0.154 ns/e    2.1x

  Scope beyond ACO: 780 bit-field declarations in the AMD and NIR
