+     A BIT_FIELD_REF of an integral container is just a shift and a mask,
+     which is exactly what the body of this function already emits, so
+     lowering it unconditionally costs nothing and lets the loop
+     vectorize.  Concretely this is the shape that Mesa's bit-packed IR
+     nodes produce (ACO's Temp id_:24/reg_class:8, nir_instr's flag
+     bitfields, ac_surface descriptors -- 780 bitfield declarations in
+     the AMD/NIR headers alone):
